<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konstantin von Kaufmann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1882">1882</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Konstantin Petrovich von Kaufmann</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Konstantin von Kaufmann was a Russian general and the first governor-general of Russian Turkestan, from its creation in 1867 until his death in 1882. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> he directed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> in 1868, made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> after campaigns in 1868 and 1873, and annexed the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> in 1876. He ruled the region with wide powers and encouraged trade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> growing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konstantin-von-kaufmann.jpg" alt="Konstantin von Kaufmann" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Рисовал К. О. Брож, гравировал Л. А. Серяков · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Von_Kaufman_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kaufmann was born on 2 March 1818, the second of four sons of Lieutenant General Peter Feodorovich von Kaufmann. His family, German in origin, had served the tsars for over a century and had become Russian Orthodox. He graduated from the Nikolayev Engineering Institute as a military engineer, served in the Caucasus campaigns, commanded the sappers at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> in 1855 and settled its surrender terms with General William Fenwick Williams. From 1861 he helped the war minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-milyutin">Dmitry Milyutin</a> reorganise the army, and from 1864 he was Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a>, where the state was expropriating the Polish aristocracy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His campaign of 1868 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. In the Khivan campaign of 1873 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a>, where the slaves had risen as his army approached; he made the khan, Muhammad Rahim Khan II, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and demanded that he abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, which he did. In 1875 he defeated Nasreddin, who had led an uprising against the previous khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>, and the rest of the khanate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferghana-valley">Ferghana Valley</a> was annexed. The reception of his envoys by Sher Ali Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> was a main cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war">Second Anglo-Afghan War</a>.</p><p>The temporary statutes under which Turkestan was governed gave him unusual independence: he negotiated with neighbouring states, set taxes, controlled the budget and could confirm or revoke death sentences of the military courts, ruling through a military bureaucracy laid loosely over a largely unreformed local administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kaufmann also had the new territory studied. He brought in Alexei and Olga Fedchenko, whose three expeditions between 1868 and 1872 documented its natural history, with statisticians, the war artist Vasily Vereshchagin and later the educationalist Nikolai Ostroumov, set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> branch of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> scientific society and showed the findings at the 1872 Moscow technical exhibition. He could not persuade the government to back all his plans of further conquest. He died in office on 16 May 1882, and several plants, among them Tulipa kaufmanniana, are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Konstantin Petrovich von Kaufmann” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Russian forces took Tashkent in 1865, the Turkestan Governorate-General was created in 1867 under General <b>Konstantin von Kaufmann</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/turkestan-governorate-general">Turkestan Governorate-General</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dęblin, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.57,21.86&amp;year=1859" aria-label="Dęblin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Konstantin von Kaufmann"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4569 5692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4569 5692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.57,21.86&amp;year=1859">What was here in 1859? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
